On Fri, 09 Jun 2006 00:35:59 GMT, Jack Bolsen wrote:

>A word here about weapon enhancements for wizards: I must respectfully 
>disagree with those who say that wizards shouldn't bother to enhance 
>their quarterstaff skills. Unless you get an early Magicbane or have 
>ridiculous amount of magic power then you must have some offensive 
>capability while recouping power. If you never enhance quarterstaff 
>skills you will be woefully vulnerable in the early/middlegame. It is 
>better to survive now than to worry about what happens to wasted skill 
>slots later.

  A quarterstaff does a whole 3.5 damage, vs 3.0 damage for an elven
dagger.  A quarterstaff prevents you from wearing a shield. (I found a
+4 elven shield once.  That's 6 points of AC unusable with a
quarterstaff.)  Quarterstaff has a range of 1.  Daggers have a range of
Str/2.  Using ranged attacks early will also prepare you to use ranged
spells later.

  So, expert quarterstaff does 5.5 damage, at range one, without a
shield.  Expert thrown daggers do average 10 damage from range 1 to
range Str/2.  Expert thrown daggers double strength damage bonus.  (At
Str 18 that's 2 more points of damage than quarterstaff.)  Expert thrown
daggers double the damage from rings of increase damage.  Expert thrown
daggers hit monsters without getting hit back.  (There's very little in
NetHack that's better than that.)

  You can also throw daggers at range one.  So you can still do more
damage at melee range with daggers than with a quarterstaff.

  For those that can't stand killing monsters at range, Expert wielded
elven dagger does 5.0 damage, only 0.5 less than quarterstaff. 

  Yes, the starting quarterstaff is +1, but enchanted daggers aren't
that rare.  Sometimes highly enchanted.  (I found a stack of five +4
daggers once.  Since they didn't stack with my other dagger I thought
they must be +0.  I did wonder why monsters died so quick to my thrown
daggers.)

  So give up a shield and doing more damage at range with thrown daggers
to do a little more damage at range one with a quarterstaff.  (Note:
expert Quarterstaff takes 5 skill slots and expert daggers takes 6.
Doing both of them is really not an option.)

>
>So I opted for better survival. 

